course: dictionary
lesson: 15424
title: Stake: a post, a bet, or a share
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Stake is one of them. A stake is a strong, pointed post pushed into the ground. «Dad hammered a stake into the soil to hold up the bean plant.» Here the stake is a wooden post that keeps the plant standing straight.
explain: Stake can also mean money, or something valuable, that you risk in a bet or game. «Tom put a stake of five pounds on the race.» If his horse wins, he gets more money back. If it loses, he loses his stake.
explain: A stake can be a share in something. «She has a stake in the family shop.» If the shop does well, she gains. If it does badly, she loses too. Check the words around the word to see which meaning fits.
fact: A stake is a strong, pointed post pushed into the ground.
fact: A stake is money or something valuable that you risk in a bet or game.
fact: «High stakes» means a lot can be won or lost.
fact: «At stake» means at risk, able to be won or lost.
fact: As a verb, «stake» can mean to bet or to claim, as in «stake a claim».
exercise: «Dad hammered a stake into the soil to hold up the bean plant.» What is a stake here? | A loud noise | A kind of cake | *A strong, pointed post | A type of plant food
exercise: «Mia put her last coin on the spinning wheel as her stake.» What does stake mean? | *Money risked in a bet or game | A prize for winning a race | A friend who helps | A pointed stick in the ground
exercise: «The final is at stake, so every goal matters.» What does at stake mean? | *At risk, able to be won or lost | Already finished | Happening far away | Hidden in a box
test: «Sam has a stake in his uncle's bakery, so he shares the profits when it does well.» What does stake mean here? | A loud shout | A sweet snack | *A share or interest in something | A pointed post for plants
test: Which phrase means that a lot is at risk? | Low stakes | *High stakes | Pull up stakes | Stake out
exam: Which sentence uses stake as a verb, meaning to claim something? | Her stake in the club is small | She hammered a stake into the ground | The stake fell over in the wind | *She decided to stake a claim on the empty field
